2026-09-28 — K01/K01b: cache-maintenance intervention and negative control.

This is a private QEMU experiment, not a production-emulator update or K13.
The matching upstream 11.1.1 source/build from qemu-instruction-publication.out
is reused. All five earlier store/decode/plugin modifications were removed.
An unmodified rebuild exactly reproduces the previous pristine binary hash:
89444ea0967926a65da85fe622db334de2ea4ba496bf09af41832a66ffcb2acf.
The old ordered-observer executable is preserved separately. No clone, new VM
disk, installed-emulator change, compiler change or active-build edit occurred.

Hypothesis and intervention:
  System-mode AArch64 IC IVAU was ARM_CP_NOP. Following the guest's stores,
  the candidate resolves an accessible RAM cache line through the current MMU
  and invalidates its physical translation-block range. The existing access
  checks remain; no address, instruction encoding or store width is singled out.
  Aliases use the same physical RAM identity. The existing user-mode callback
  is unchanged. This uses the existing QEMU invalidation mechanism; it does not
  introduce a second translation cache or modify the kernel/test protocol.

The guest's actual instruction-cache operation supplies the post-store boundary,
not a second callback on just the four-byte slow store. This is a wider causal
experiment, but NOT yet a complete cache-maintenance implementation. Inaccessible
and non-RAM addresses retain the old no-op behavior; IALLU/IALLUIS, AArch32,
CTR.DIC-elided maintenance, fault semantics and other machines are unaudited.
The underlying generic pre-store invalidation race is not eliminated for code
which does not execute this cache-maintenance operation. Do not deploy this as
an all-target/all-path QEMU fix or silently replace the locked runtime lane.

Negative control:
  Same callback, MMU lookup, barrier and translation-block-ending shape, but
  remove only tb_invalidate_phys_range. This distinguishes the invalidation
  effect from merely adding a helper call, barrier or block boundary. No plugin,
  diagnostic store predicate, guest assertion or timeout is changed in either.

Unchanged existing reductions on BOTH retained Images:
                                             reference       subject
  Cache-maintenance candidate, userspace     6 PASS          6 PASS
  Lookup/barrier-only control, userspace     3 PASS/3 FAIL   3 PASS/3 FAIL
  Cache-maintenance candidate, static-key    3 PASS          3 PASS
The three concurrent branch modes fail again only when invalidation is removed;
memory-gate, locked-MOV and handshake controls still pass. Static-key coverage
uses the previously signed module, including its memory-gate control. These
results reproduce a required cache-publication effect, not the original NULL
fault's complete cause.

Unchanged COMPLETE sched_ext program, all 23 tests, no filter:
                                             reference       subject
  Pristine private QEMU                      23/23 PASS      TIMEOUT
  Cache-maintenance candidate                23/23 PASS      TIMEOUT
Both use the existing 120-second inner budget, 135-second cleanup watchdog and
195-second boot watchdog. Both Images boot and shut down normally; that boot
PASS is not the subject program's verdict. No kernel NULL fault is observed in
these four flights. The old subject reaches reload_loop on the pristine run and
prog_run on the candidate before timeout. This is not a timing-performance
measurement: the uniform kernel build was concurrently using four host workers.
These are the retained older Images, not the pending 7,002-object composition.
Thus the original intermittent fault and full subject program are NOT CLOSED.

